Smart Pointer
• Motivation
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <sys/resource.h>
using namespace std;
// get memory footprint
long get_mem() {
struct rusage is;
getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF, &is);
return is.ru_maxrss; // in KB
}
void subA() { 
int a; int* pa = &a; 
}
void subB() {
int* pa = new int;
// delete pa; // free memory space, no automatic deallocation
}
void subC() { unique_ptr<int> pa = make_unique<int>(); }
int main( void ) {
long mem_base = get_mem();
cout << "Memory base usage: " << mem_base << endl;
for( long i=0; i<10000000; i++ ) {
subA();
if( i%1000000 == 0) cout << "Addt'l allocation: " << get_mem() - mem_base << endl;
}
return 0;
}
C++
70
subA creates a local integer variable. Itself as well as the local pointer to it get
deleted, once we leave the context.
In subB the local pointer pa points to a newly requested memory location of size
integer. The local pointer variable is deleted as we leave the context. However,
the memory location allocated with new is not freed up. Missing the explicit
delete command, there is no reference to this memory location available to the
program – a memory leak is created.
subC uses a smart pointer, that keeps track of its usage. The original assignment
increases the number of references to the allocated memory location by one.
Leaving the context, the deletion of the local smart pointer reduces the reference
count to zero, which automatically triggers memory management routine to free-
up the memory resource.

