move()
71
Unique pointers assure that an object can only be owned by a single pointer. No 
other pointer can reference the same object.
The move() method is used to explicitly move ownership of an object from one 
pointer to another. If an object is owned by a temporary unique pointer, the object 
can be transferred using a return expression without explicitly requiring a call to 
move().
As best practice, objects handled by unique pointers should be created with 
make_unique.

