Smart Pointer
• Shared Pointer
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
using namespace std;
void show( shared_ptr<string>& p0, shared_ptr<string>& p1 ) {
if( p0 != nullptr ) cout << "Shared pointer p0: '" << *p0 << "'" << endl;
if( p1 != nullptr ) cout << "Shared pointer p1: '" << *p1 << "'" << endl;
cout << " Ptr of p0 use count: " << p0.use_count() << endl;
cout << " Ptr of p1 use count: " << p1.use_count() << endl << endl;
}
int main( void )
{
shared_ptr<string> ptr0 = make_shared<string>("shared");
shared_ptr<string> ptr1;
show(ptr0, ptr1);
cout << "Duplicating reference to object ..." << endl;
ptr1 = ptr0;
show(ptr0, ptr1);
ptr0 = make_shared<string>("something new");
show(ptr0, ptr1);
return 0;
}
C++
Wir nutzen call‐by‐reference, um eine 
Erhöhung der Pointerzahl zu vermeiden.
72
Shared pointers shared_ptr allow object ownership by multiple pointers. An 
internal counter keeps track whether there is still a pointer referencing it. If the 
count is down to zero, the object is automatically deleted.

