© T. Gemmeke, IDS, RWTH
Smart Pointer
• Weak Pointer
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
using namespace std;
void show( shared_ptr<string>& p0, weak_ptr<string>& p1 ) {
if( p0 != nullptr ) cout << "Shared pointer p0: '" << *p0 << "'" << endl;
if( !p1.expired() ) cout << "Weak pointer p1: '" << *(p1.lock()) << "'" << endl;
cout << " Ptr of p0 use count: " << p0.use_count() << endl;
cout << " Ptr of p1 use count: " << p1.use_count() << endl << endl;
}
int main( void )
{
shared_ptr<string> ptr0 = make_shared<string>("shared");
weak_ptr<string> ptr1;
show(ptr0, ptr1);
cout << "Duplicating reference to object ..." << endl;
ptr1 = ptr0;
show(ptr0, ptr1);
ptr0 = make_shared<string>("something new");
show(ptr0, ptr1);
return 0;
}
C++
Einen weak pointer zu de‐referenzieren ist etwas 
kompliziert. Es benötigt eine temporäre Kopie der Referenz. 
Diese wird durch die Methode lock() erreicht.
obj1 
obj2 
obj3 
obj1 
obj4 
obj2 
obj3 
73
Weak pointers weak_ptr can also point to objects owned by shared pointers.
However, they do not add to the count metric. If no shared pointer is referencing
the object, it is deleted. The weak pointer is then pointing to an invalid address.
In the figure, example obj1 points to obj2, which points itself to obj3. If obj3 used
a shared_ptr to point back to obj2, the re-assignment of obj1 to obj4 would lose
any reference to the other two objects. However, the system could not identify the
two objects being obsolete – a memory leak is created. With the indicated weak
pointer, obj2 looses any reference to it. Once obj2 is deleted also obj3 is up for
removal.

