In C++, we can have different declarations for the same symbol of a function –
this is called polymorphism. In c this is not possible. As an example, we want to 
compute the root of a number, which frequently is simply the square root. In this 
case, a single input parameter is required as given in the first declaration of the 
function root. 
In c, only one of the declarations of root be included at a given time. 
Activating both in C++ triggers the compiler to select the appropriate declaration 
during compile-time. The decision is based on the number of provided parameters
given in the function call. As this decision is done prior to execution we call this 
static polymorphism.

