A variable can also hold a set (i.e., a collection of objects) - we call such variable
a container. In the simplest form this can be a vector or an array, that is a linear
arrangement of the objects in memory. We will learn more about other data
structures in the later chapters.
The 1st for-loop iterates through all elements of the container. The current
element is copied into elm. This is basically a for-each operation.
In the 2nd example, elm contains a copy-by-reference of the element in the
container, i.e., the int values in the vector cannot be modified. It uses a copy by
reference, which is good practice especially for large objects such that no
temporary copy has to be created. In principle, elements of a vector could now be
modified.
The additional type specifier const prevents this again in the 3rd loop.
The 4th loop uses an explicit for_each expression.
Finally, the 5th loop uses a classic for construct, but with an iterator variable. An 
alternative would be using the index:
for(size_t i=0; i < v.size(); i++) …

